<description>
Draw the missing diagonal connector segment between each pair of same-color rectangular objects.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- Every foreground color appears as exactly two separated solid rectangular 4-connected objects.
- The two rectangles of each color are diagonally offset with facing corners on a 45-degree line.

Rule:
- Keep all existing rectangles unchanged.
- For each foreground color, connect the lower facing corner of the upper rectangle to the upper facing corner of the lower rectangle.
- Paint only the intervening background pixels on that diagonal, excluding the two endpoint pixels already in the rectangles.

Key insight:
- Same-color rectangles define the endpoints; the output completes the open diagonal between their nearest facing corners.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "each pair of same-color solid rectangular objects",
  "dst": "connect to the other rectangle of that color",
  "path": "45-degree diagonal between the pair's facing corners",
  "trajectory": "paint the open segment between endpoints with that color",
  "transform": "keep the original rectangles unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 16x16, background color 7
  foreground_pairs: 3-5 distinct colors, each appearing as exactly 2 same-size solid rectangles
  rectangle_sizes:
    per_pair: both rectangles identical; either 2x2 or 3x3
    mix: 1 pair is 3x3, rest are 2x2 [observed]
  pair_geometry:
    vertical_separation: upper rect bottom row < lower rect top row (no row overlap)
    horizontal_separation: column ranges do not overlap (diagonally offset)
    facing_corners: the nearest corners of the two rects lie on an exact 45-degree diagonal
    diagonal_gap: 2-6 pixels between facing corners (exclusive of endpoints)
    direction: mix of upper-left-to-lower-right and upper-right-to-lower-left across pairs
  constraints:
    no_overlap: rectangles and diagonals never overlap each other
    diagonals_on_bg: every diagonal pixel falls on a background cell in the input
    min_bbox_gap: >=2 cells between any two rectangles (any color pair)
  output:
    same_size: grid unchanged except diagonal connectors added
    connector: for each color, paint the open 45-degree diagonal segment between facing corners (endpoints excluded, they are already in the rectangles)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose 3-5 foreground colors and assign each a rectangle size (one 3x3, rest 2x2).
    2. For each color, pick a diagonal direction (LR or RL) and a gap length (2-6).
       Compute facing-corner positions, then derive both rectangle bboxes from those corners + sizes.
    3. Place all rectangles and their diagonals on the 16x16 grid.
       Validate no rectangle or diagonal overlaps any other rectangle or diagonal, and all stay in bounds.
       Retry placement on conflict (use random corner offsets within grid).
    4. The output grid is the placed rectangles + diagonals.
    5. Remove the diagonal pixels (set to bg) to produce the input grid.
```
</generation_spec>
